# DET MCP (v1)

Use the **det** MCP server for inspect and dry-run planning inside Cursor. Prefer MCP
tools over shelling out to `det` when exploring pipelines, lake partitions, prune
plans, or scaffold/dbt previews.

## v1 policy (dry-run / read-only)

**Allowed via MCP:** `list_*`, `describe_pipeline`, `describe_model`, `read_manifest`,
`diff_partitions`, `sample_raw`, `validate_sample`, `sample_bronze`,
`diagnose_pipeline`, `check`, `query_analytics`, `cube_meta`, `cube_load`,
`schema_from_sample_dry_run`,
`mapper_from_diff_dry_run`,
`airflow_health`, `list_airflow_dags`, `list_airflow_dag_runs`,
`describe_airflow_det_env`, `preview_backfill_conf`, `migrate_dry_run`,
`list_runs`, `summarize_runs`,
`prune_dry_run`, `dbt_dry_run`, `scaffold_dbt_dry_run`, `init_pipeline_dry_run`,
`list_approvals`, `describe_approval`,
`det://` resources, and prompts `det_ops` / `det_new_source` / `det_migrate` /
`det_dbt` / `det_airflow` (skill playbooks).

Dry-runs that preview a write include `approval_plan` (`command`, `argv`,
`plan_digest`). MCP never creates `.det/approvals/` files — that is `det approve`.

Approvals are **audit and intent-binding, not authorization** (the same shell can
run `det approve`). The record is guaranteed to describe the command that runs:
every flag that changes what or where data is written is in `plan_digest`, and an
unrecognized flag is rejected as `approval_unbound_flag`. Practical consequence —
**re-approve whenever a flag changes**, including `--lake-path`, `--set`,
`--full-refresh`, `--target`, `--ingestion`. Pipeline refs and intervals are
canonicalized, so the ref form (`noaa.storm_events` vs `noaa/storm_events` vs the
YAML path) and the interval form (`2026-08-06` vs `2026-08-06T00:00:00+00:00`)
do not affect the digest.

Sample size for `sample_*` / `validate_sample` / `schema_from_sample_dry_run` is
`limit` (default **5** for sample/validate, up to **50**). `diagnose_pipeline`
uses `sample_limit` the same way.

**Not in MCP v1 — use CLI deliberately:** `det extract` / `load` / `run`,
`det migrate` (write; use MCP `migrate_dry_run` to preview), `det prune --apply`,
Airflow DagRun triggers, non-dry-run `scaffold-dbt` / `init-pipeline`, writing
schema or mapper files from generate previews. Structure check is MCP `check`
(same payload as `det check --json`; CI still runs the CLI).

Airflow inspect uses `DET_AIRFLOW_BASE_URL` / `USER` / `PASSWORD` (Compose
defaults to `http://localhost:8080`). Never trigger DagRuns via MCP.

Cube Core inspect uses `DET_CUBE_BASE_URL` / `DET_CUBE_API_SECRET` (Compose
defaults to `http://localhost:4000` / `cube/.env`). Never invent gold/ops metric
SQL when Cube is down — return the error and `make cube-up`.

Paths are sandboxed under `DET_PROJECT_ROOT` (default cwd / project root).

## MCP vs CLI

| Goal | Prefer |
| --- | --- |
| List pipelines / describe config | MCP `list_pipelines` / `describe_pipeline` |
| After YAML/schema edits | MCP `check` (same as `det check --json`) |
| dbt catalog | MCP `list_models` / `describe_model` |
| Gold / fleet metrics | MCP `cube_meta` / `cube_load` (`make cube-up`; Cube Core on :4000) |
| Silver or ops row detail | MCP `query_analytics` (`warehouse=analytics` or `ops`, LIMIT cap) |
| Debug missing raw / bronze / schema | MCP `diagnose_pipeline` (or `diff_partitions` / `sample_raw` / `validate_sample`) |
| Debug a failed or slow extract/load | MCP `list_runs` / `summarize_runs` (or `det runs`) — observability, not landed-data truth |
| Sample bronze rows | MCP `sample_bronze` (filesystem JSONL or DuckDB/Postgres `LIMIT`) |
| Draft schema from sample | MCP `schema_from_sample_dry_run` then write after confirm |
| Draft migrate mapper | MCP `mapper_from_diff_dry_run` then register + `det migrate` |
| Airflow health / DAG runs | MCP `airflow_health` / `list_airflow_dags` / `list_airflow_dag_runs` |
| Preview backfill conf | MCP `preview_backfill_conf` then Compose/CLI trigger if approved |
| Preview migrate | MCP `migrate_dry_run` then **ask user**; `det approve`; later CLI `det migrate --approval` |
| Preview prune | MCP `prune_dry_run` then **ask user**; `det approve`; later CLI `--apply --approval` |
| Preview dbt/scaffold | MCP `*_dry_run` then **ask user**; `det approve`; later CLI `--approval` without `--dry-run` |
| Mutating extract/load / DagRun trigger | CLI / Airflow UI only — after explicit user approval |

**Agent rule:** never chain a dry-run preview into a writing CLI command in the same
turn. Show the plan/result and wait for the user to approve. Operator: `det approve`.
Agent write (later turn): `--approval <id>` when required. Run the writing command
with exactly the flags in the approved `argv` — anything else fails the gate.

An approval whose run crashed stays `claimed` and never expires, so it will not
appear in the default listing. Find it with `list_approvals` `status="claimed"`
(or `det list-approvals --status claimed`). Recovery is a fresh approval, or the
operator running `det approval-release <id> --force` after confirming the worker
is dead. Never delete the record by hand, and never suggest releasing one whose
run may still be running — that reopens the double-write window.

Source plugins are discovered from `src/det/sources/<provider>/<source>.py` (`name`
must match). Do not register them in `plugins.py`. Migrate mappers use `@mapper` on
the function in that module.

## dlt boundaries

dlt is extraction tooling only. Never suggest `dlt.pipeline` / `pipeline.run` for
bronze landing, dlt load state, or normalizer unnesting. DET owns validation, meta,
and writers (`write_jsonl_partition`, `write_duckdb_table`, `write_postgres_table`).
See also `dlt-boundaries` rule.
